WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Human error is a normal feature of complex work.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:06.070
Even skilled, careful people miss steps, misread signals,

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:09.120
choose based on stale assumptions, or act too quickly under pressure.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.680
A strong performance system does not pretend errors will disappear.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.760
It designs work so mistakes are less likely, easier to catch,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.180
and less likely to become serious failures.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.360
Checklists and standard operating procedures support repeatable execution.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.840
They do not replace expertise; they protect it.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.310
A good checklist helps ensure that known critical steps are not missed when attention is

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.291
limited or pressure is high.

00:00:29.291 --> 00:00:33.280
Procedures also make expectations visible so teams can improve the process instead of

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:34.680
relying on memory.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.840
Pre-mortems, decision journals, and peer review improve decision quality.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.591
A pre-mortem asks what could go wrong before action begins.

00:00:40.591 --> 00:00:43.210
A decision journal captures the reasoning, assumptions,

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.540
and expected outcomes at the time of the decision.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.475
Peer review adds another perspective that may catch blind spots, unclear logic,

00:00:49.475 --> 00:00:51.160
or overlooked risks.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.205
High-risk actions deserve deliberate friction.

00:00:53.205 --> 00:00:55.640
Slow down when the cost of being wrong is high.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.235
Separate planning from execution.

00:00:57.235 --> 00:00:59.240
Confirm scope before irreversible steps.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.075
Use two-person review where appropriate.

00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:03.425
Design systems that catch mistakes before they matter.

00:01:03.425 --> 00:01:07.110
Reliability comes from respecting human limits and building controls around them.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.790
Error management works best when the team can discuss mistakes without turning every

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:11.760
review into blame.

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.270
The useful question is not only who made the error, but what made the error likely,

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.355
hard to detect, or easy to amplify.

00:01:17.355 --> 00:01:20.675
That shift helps teams improve the system while still preserving accountability for

00:01:20.675 --> 00:01:21.440
careful work.
